<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nasiruddin Nusrat Shah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 1519–1533</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Nusrat Shah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nasiruddin Nusrat Shah was sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> from 1519 to 1533, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-husain-shah">Alauddin Husain Shah</a>. He supported the Afghan chiefs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> and lost to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ghaghra">Battle of Ghaghra</a> in 1529, then made peace. A patron of Bengali literature, he built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bara-sona-mosque">Bara Sona Mosque</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Gaur</a> and the Qadam Rasul shrine. He was assassinated in 1533.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nasiruddin-nusrat-shah.jpg" alt="Nasiruddin Nusrat Shah" width="320" height="159" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:LouisAragon (uploader) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:INDIA,_Islamic_Sultanates._Bengal._Nasir_al-Din_Nusrat._AD_1519-1531.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
